{
  "text": "For every finite-dimensional real inner-product space E, equipped with its induced norm, norm topology, and Borel σ-algebra, let λ_E denote the canonical Lebesgue measure normalized by orthonormal coordinates. For every real-valued function V : E → ℝ and every real number m > 0, assume that V is genuinely Fréchet differentiable over ℝ at every point of E and is m-strongly convex on all of E. Explicitly, for every x,y ∈ E and every pair of real numbers a,b ≥ 0 with a+b = 1, assume\n\nV(a x + b y) ≤ a V(x) + b V(y) − (m/2) a b ‖x−y‖².\n\nThen the function x ↦ exp(−V(x)) is integrable with respect to λ_E. This conclusion means both that the function is almost everywhere strongly measurable and that the nonnegative integral of its absolute value is finite. It is a genuine integrability assertion, not merely a statement about a real-valued, totalized Bochner integral expression.\n\nThe strong-convexity domain is the entire space E, which is convex. All weights and scalar multiplications in the displayed inequality are real, and the coefficient of the squared-distance correction is exactly m/2. The exponential is the positive real exponential. Canonical volume is not an arbitrary measure-space parameter. No positive-dimension or nontriviality assumption is imposed; dimension zero is allowed, with canonical volume equal to unit mass on the one-point space. Completeness is supplied by finite dimensionality, rather than imposed as a separate hypothesis. The differentiability premise asserts existence of an actual derivative everywhere, not a value assigned by a totalized derivative API.",
